-## Why this is claimed hard
+## Why it is Track B
 
-Theorem 1 supplies the native parameter regime used here: `r2=1`, `r3=2`, and `s=n>=17`, with both coefficient matrices highly nonsingular.  Section 4 explains that its asymptotic constant can be zero and that positivity requires nonsingular real and p-adic points; the paper supplies no polynomial-time algorithm for finding a bounded integral point.  Our planted point is itself a nonsingular rational/real point, so its existence does not depend on an unexecuted local-solubility claim.
+Theorem 1.1 applies at (r_2=1,r_3=2,s=nge17), provided both coefficient
+matrices are highly nonsingular. Section 4 explains the important limitation:
+the asymptotic constant can vanish unless nonsingular real and (p)-adic points
+exist. The planted rank-three integral point supplies such local points directly.
+The theorem is an asymptotic counting result, not a computational-hardness claim
+and not a certificate-finding algorithm.
 
-This is a Track A claim about the generated distribution, not a claim that Theorem 1 proves computational hardness.  The shipping signed-permutation language has about `2^148.663` candidates.  A structure-aware sample found `0/200000` valid candidates.  A permutation-CSP branch-and-bound with exact residual bounds exhausted 250,000 nodes on each of eight seeds—2,000,000 nodes in 10.719532 seconds—with `0/8` successes.  Column-scale, joint-greedy, and 24-by-1200 local-restart attacks also scored `0/8`.
+An efficient mechanical method does exist for this generated subclass. Sorting
+the quadratic coefficients recovers every magnitude. The remaining two equations
+are a two-dimensional signed subset sum, solved by meet-in-the-middle in
+(O(nlog n+2^{(n-1)/2})) time and (O(2^{(n-1)/2})) memory. On eight shipping
+instances it solved 8/8, enumerating 2,097,152 states and performing 9,405,614
+exact additions/subtractions in 3.292730 seconds.
 
-Two tempting easy constructions were avoided.  Restricting every magnitude to one makes the cubic equations linear in signs and makes the quadratic equation sign-blind.  Solving for one final coefficient makes that coordinate a conspicuous magnitude outlier.  The signed-permutation language retains both powers, and exchangeable all-coordinate lattice mixing removes the distinguished final coordinate.
+The compressed route is 245 exact operations at (n=35). The quadratic
+coefficients are an arithmetic progression in hidden rank. After ranks are
+decoded, order cubic columns by magnitude and take determinants of consecutive
+columns around the cycle. Each planted sign occurs twice in their product, so
+the hidden common (2	imes2) map contributes only one global determinant sign;
+(x_0>0) fixes the final global sign. Recognizing that invariant replaces more
+than a million mechanical integer operations.
 
